## Overview

This system runs fast-action multiplayer matches over UDP with an authoritative server: clients feel responsive via local prediction, but the server is the single source of truth for game state and outcomes. The key insight is to **treat “real-time state sync” and “player assignment” as two different systems**: a latency-critical data plane (UDP game servers) and a consistency/availability-oriented control plane (matchmaking + fleet allocation).

Most teams fail by trying to “make UDP reliable like TCP” or by over-trusting clients. This design stays elegant by using UDP for what it’s good at (low-latency, unordered delivery) while adding only the minimal reliability needed for *inputs and critical events*. Everything else is snapshots, deltas, and time.

## What Makes This Hard

Naive implementations get trapped by time ambiguity: packets arrive late, out of order, or not at all, yet players demand instant feedback and fair outcomes. If you simply apply inputs “when received,” fast players get advantages and slow players get punished; if you trust the client for hit results, cheating becomes trivial.

The hard problem is **reconciling a responsive client experience with an authoritative timeline** under loss/jitter. The second hard problem is **match quality vs. queue time** without building a “matchmaking science project.”


